304
00:18:54,110 --> 00:18:56,330
And then you can
press the A key,

305
00:18:56,330 --> 00:18:58,970
and it will
automatically install

306
00:18:58,970 --> 00:19:03,830
the Expo Go utilities it
needs on your emulator

307
00:19:03,830 --> 00:19:07,570
and run the app on
the emulator for you.

308
00:19:07,570 --> 00:19:11,050
Same with iOS.

309
00:19:11,050 --> 00:19:12,930
It will give you a
prompt in the terminal

310
00:19:12,930 --> 00:19:14,790
to be able to run it
on an iOS simulator.

311
00:19:14,790 --> 00:19:21,730
But on iOS, you would download
Xcode from the App Store

312
00:19:21,730 --> 00:19:24,410
and/or from the
website directly.

313
00:19:24,410 --> 00:19:29,750
And then through Xcode, you
download iOS simulators.

314
00:19:32,370 --> 00:19:34,950
I'm going to go ahead
and stop it for now.

315
00:19:39,450 --> 00:19:41,380
The one downside of
running these emulators

316
00:19:41,380 --> 00:19:45,880
is it's pretty heavy
computationally.

317
00:19:45,880 --> 00:19:49,460
So if you don't have
a beefy computer,

318
00:19:49,460 --> 00:19:51,980
your computer might
struggle to run those

319
00:19:51,980 --> 00:19:56,093
at the same time
as everything else.

320
00:19:56,093 --> 00:19:57,260
Again, one of the benefits--

321
00:19:57,260 --> 00:20:00,460
I recommend going with a
physical device with Expo Go,

322
00:20:00,460 --> 00:20:02,440
just because that's
the easiest way.

323
00:20:02,440 --> 00:20:07,100
And you don't have to
worry about extra software

324
00:20:07,100 --> 00:20:08,080
on your computer.

325
00:20:13,020 --> 00:20:16,120
So that's everything we
need to get going there.

326
00:20:18,700 --> 00:20:21,120
When you're running
on your EC2 instance,

327
00:20:21,120 --> 00:20:26,680
you will be able to access the
web version of your expo app.

328
00:20:26,680 --> 00:20:30,180
If you are wanting to
target mobile specifically

329
00:20:30,180 --> 00:20:34,580
for your app, or even
exclusively mobile,

330
00:20:34,580 --> 00:20:41,660
you will need to clone your
repo to your local computer

331
00:20:41,660 --> 00:20:42,800
and run it locally.

332
00:20:42,800 --> 00:20:46,180
There isn't a way
to do mobile dev

333
00:20:46,180 --> 00:20:50,460
with Expo on Amazon EC2 images.

334
00:20:50,460 --> 00:21:01,540
The reason for that is
because to run Expo Go

335
00:21:01,540 --> 00:21:03,720
and connect to your
local Metro server,

336
00:21:03,720 --> 00:21:06,920
you have to be on
the same network.

337
00:21:13,500 --> 00:21:17,540
And I have tested this on
Blue-- or not BlueZone.

338
00:21:17,540 --> 00:21:18,760
It's not BlueZone anymore.

339
00:21:18,760 --> 00:21:22,300
It's called eduroam, I think.

340
00:21:22,300 --> 00:21:26,780
I did install or connect
to eduroam on my laptop

341
00:21:26,780 --> 00:21:36,460
and on my phone, and I was able
to run Expo Go through my phone

342
00:21:36,460 --> 00:21:38,890
on the eduroam.

343
00:21:38,890 --> 00:21:40,470
I don't know how
that works or how

344
00:21:40,470 --> 00:21:43,610
they are able to see each
other on that network,

345
00:21:43,610 --> 00:21:45,070
but it does work.

346
00:21:45,070 --> 00:21:48,250
But it won't work on
your Amazon EC2 instance.

347
00:21:52,110 --> 00:21:54,830
OK, so I've got
that popped open.

348
00:21:54,830 --> 00:21:59,230
Let's look at routing
at this point.

349
00:21:59,230 --> 00:22:03,030
So we'll pop open this.

350
00:22:03,030 --> 00:22:06,030
It talks about
file-based routing.

351
00:22:06,030 --> 00:22:09,910
And what this means is
usually with React Navigation,

352
00:22:09,910 --> 00:22:11,470
or other web
navigation, you would

353
00:22:11,470 --> 00:22:15,670
have to set up some sort
of routing configuration

354
00:22:15,670 --> 00:22:20,990
so you have extra files that
define your navigation tree.

355
00:22:20,990 --> 00:22:26,750
In the case of expo-router,
you don't have to do that.

356
00:22:26,750 --> 00:22:32,710
You're going to just lay out
your navigation tree in the file

357
00:22:32,710 --> 00:22:39,630
system, and it will interpret
that and build the file trees

358
00:22:39,630 --> 00:22:40,910
for you.

359
00:22:40,910 --> 00:22:43,010
While we've got
this Expo App open,

360
00:22:43,010 --> 00:22:48,070
I'll just step through
what a router layout might

361
00:22:48,070 --> 00:22:52,870
look like for the demo
example that Expo gives us

362
00:22:52,870 --> 00:22:54,350
when we start it up.

363
00:22:54,350 --> 00:22:59,950
We've got the _layout.tsx
inside this (tabs) folder.

364
00:22:59,950 --> 00:23:02,950
The parentheses name
of a folder here

365
00:23:02,950 --> 00:23:11,150
is how they denote a nested
router inside of the app folder.

366
00:23:11,150 --> 00:23:15,250
You could put all of your pages
just directly in the app folder.

367
00:23:15,250 --> 00:23:19,230
But if you have
nested router logic,

368
00:23:19,230 --> 00:23:23,950
then you can organize those
into folders like this.

369
00:23:23,950 --> 00:23:28,550
So inside of their tabs,
they've got their _layout.tsx.

370
00:23:28,550 --> 00:23:33,370
And I don't know why this is
highlighting it red this time,

371
00:23:33,370 --> 00:23:35,950
but it is.

372
00:23:35,950 --> 00:23:39,992
But anyway, they just
put a tab navigator,

373
00:23:39,992 --> 00:23:42,200
and that's what we saw on
the other screen, those two

374
00:23:42,200 --> 00:23:43,140
bottom tabs.

375
00:23:43,140 --> 00:23:49,960
And they defined what screens
those tabs navigate to.

376
00:23:49,960 --> 00:23:52,440
The default one is index.

377
00:23:52,440 --> 00:23:58,560
And so we can open
up the or index.tsx

378
00:23:58,560 --> 00:24:03,480
and see there's the source image
for that image that was up top.

379
00:24:03,480 --> 00:24:07,180
And then they've got some
ThemedViews, ThemedText.

380
00:24:07,180 --> 00:24:09,240
And that's just their
way of styling their text

381
00:24:09,240 --> 00:24:12,920
in wrapper components.

382
00:24:12,920 --> 00:24:15,680
Yeah, we don't want to
spend too much time on this,

383
00:24:15,680 --> 00:24:17,300
because I'm going to--

384
00:24:20,640 --> 00:24:21,660
that one more time.

385
00:24:25,280 --> 00:24:26,420
Pop it open.

386
00:24:30,840 --> 00:24:33,160
It's taking a second
to build the--

387
00:24:33,160 --> 00:24:34,860
compile the web assets.

388
00:24:37,700 --> 00:24:40,940
Yeah, so they say
you can try it out.

389
00:24:40,940 --> 00:24:46,120
You can go ahead and just modify
the index.tsx right inside

390
00:24:46,120 --> 00:24:50,720
of the tabs, like we
were looking at here.

391
00:24:50,720 --> 00:24:54,220
And if you wanted to use
this as a starting point,

392
00:24:54,220 --> 00:24:56,520
you're welcome to.

393
00:24:56,520 --> 00:24:59,280
That would just involve a
little bit more familiarity

394
00:24:59,280 --> 00:25:04,920
with reading the code that
they've got implemented already.

395
00:25:04,920 --> 00:25:10,280
But what I'm going to
do for demo purposes

396
00:25:10,280 --> 00:25:16,880
is npm run reset-project,
like they recommend here,

397
00:25:16,880 --> 00:25:21,440
to start my own
project structure.

398
00:25:21,440 --> 00:25:26,840
So Control-C to quit
that, and then I'll

399
00:25:26,840 --> 00:25:29,440
just paste that in there.

400
00:25:29,440 --> 00:25:33,810
No, I don't need to keep
those example files.

401
00:25:33,810 --> 00:25:35,310
OK, so it--

402
00:25:38,570 --> 00:25:42,810
I went ahead and deleted the
demo files and then started

403
00:25:42,810 --> 00:25:44,190
a new blank project.

404
00:25:44,190 --> 00:25:47,130
So let's look at
what that looks like.

405
00:25:47,130 --> 00:25:51,230
npm start-- let's
run that up again.

406
00:25:51,230 --> 00:25:52,630
We'll open it up again.

407
00:25:55,490 --> 00:25:57,330
And this is all it is.

408
00:25:57,330 --> 00:26:02,750
There's an app bar at the top
and then just a blank-- well,

409
00:26:02,750 --> 00:26:04,650
it's got a text there.

410
00:26:04,650 --> 00:26:06,690
So let's look at our
file structure now.

411
00:26:06,690 --> 00:26:08,130
We've got app.

412
00:26:08,130 --> 00:26:11,630
We've got a _layout.tsx,
which just returns a stack.

413
00:26:11,630 --> 00:26:17,130
So it's not doing any special
routing or any special layout.

414
00:26:17,130 --> 00:26:22,730
And then we've got
this index file.

415
00:26:22,730 --> 00:26:26,490
We've got this view
that's being rendered,

416
00:26:26,490 --> 00:26:29,770
and it has a text inside of it.

417
00:26:29,770 --> 00:26:34,130
So let's look at some
basic component structure

418
00:26:34,130 --> 00:26:37,650
and how this is laid out.

419
00:26:37,650 --> 00:26:43,270
First of all, the index is the
name of this page component.

420
00:26:43,270 --> 00:26:47,230
So this whole page is
the index component,

421
00:26:47,230 --> 00:26:50,970
which is a page since it's
inside of our app folder.

422
00:26:50,970 --> 00:26:56,210
We have two components inside
of our index component, View

423
00:26:56,210 --> 00:26:57,270
and a Text.

424
00:26:57,270 --> 00:27:02,050
Those are just imported from
React Native, like this.

425
00:27:02,050 --> 00:27:09,310
Text is just a tag that
takes text as children.

426
00:27:09,310 --> 00:27:15,390
So you can put any text
in here and edit that.

427
00:27:15,390 --> 00:27:18,570
And as long as your
metro server is still

428
00:27:18,570 --> 00:27:22,290
running in the
terminal, this app

429
00:27:22,290 --> 00:27:27,890
will hot reload anytime you
update your source files.

430
00:27:27,890 --> 00:27:33,440
And then the view has a prop.

431
00:27:33,440 --> 00:27:35,700
The prop is style,
and we're just

432
00:27:35,700 --> 00:27:42,180
defining the flex, which
is a way of saying,

433
00:27:42,180 --> 00:27:47,500
this component should fill
all the space that it can.

434
00:27:47,500 --> 00:27:49,900
And it should take
up-- one is like, I

435
00:27:49,900 --> 00:27:55,140
should get one share of all
the space that's remaining.

436
00:27:55,140 --> 00:27:57,200
And since it's the
only one in here,

437
00:27:57,200 --> 00:27:59,680
it gets 100% of that space.

438
00:27:59,680 --> 00:28:02,300
JustifyContent "center",
alignItems "center"

439
00:28:02,300 --> 00:28:07,160
is why this text is centered
instead of up in the top left.

440
00:28:07,160 --> 00:28:12,300
So if we delete that,
boom, it's up there.

441
00:28:12,300 --> 00:28:16,400
As you're editing
styles in your classes,

442
00:28:16,400 --> 00:28:21,100
you will probably be able
to test and say, hey, AI,

443
00:28:21,100 --> 00:28:23,400
I want this style
to look like this.

444
00:28:23,400 --> 00:28:27,620
And it's pretty good at
laying that out for you.

445
00:28:27,620 --> 00:28:30,980
For your ability to read
and understand the code,

446
00:28:30,980 --> 00:28:33,520
there are a few ways
that it might do this.

447
00:28:33,520 --> 00:28:34,920
One way is just inline.

448
00:28:34,920 --> 00:28:36,300
You can have inline
styles, which

449
00:28:36,300 --> 00:28:39,420
is what this view
has by default.

450
00:28:39,420 --> 00:28:44,940
The other way is to define
a style sheet and use those.

451
00:28:44,940 --> 00:28:46,660
So let's import.

452
00:28:46,660 --> 00:28:49,180
Let's actually
make a style sheet.

453
00:28:49,180 --> 00:28:51,500
So I'm going to do const.

454
00:28:51,500 --> 00:28:55,340
And again, for those that
haven't used JavaScript before,

455
00:28:55,340 --> 00:28:58,500
const is how you
declare a variable that

456
00:28:58,500 --> 00:29:01,380
doesn't get overwritten.

457
00:29:01,380 --> 00:29:06,680
Styles-- styles =.

458
00:29:06,680 --> 00:29:09,200
And then I'm going to
do StyleSheet.create.

459
00:29:18,460 --> 00:29:20,340
And I'm going to--

460
00:29:23,020 --> 00:29:24,780
an object in the middle.

461
00:29:24,780 --> 00:29:32,000
StyleSheet needs to be
imported from React Native.

462
00:29:32,000 --> 00:29:36,410
And then in here,
I can make a style.

463
00:29:36,410 --> 00:29:39,430
I'm going to call this the--

464
00:29:39,430 --> 00:29:41,450
let's just call it
the pageWrapper.

465
00:29:45,750 --> 00:29:54,550
And then I can get this
exact object, put it in here.

466
00:29:54,550 --> 00:29:58,810
And that is defining
that the same way.

467
00:29:58,810 --> 00:30:03,250
So now in here, we can
do styles.pageWrapper.

468
00:30:08,270 --> 00:30:12,770
And now we should have the same
styles available on this page.

469
00:30:12,770 --> 00:30:17,510
And now you can see it's
still centering the content.

470
00:30:17,510 --> 00:30:20,150
And it's taking
up the entire page

471
00:30:20,150 --> 00:30:22,730
because it's centered
across the entire page.

472
00:30:22,730 --> 00:30:26,150
So yeah, for your
ability to read this,

473
00:30:26,150 --> 00:30:30,110
using StyleSheet.create
to define a style

474
00:30:30,110 --> 00:30:34,770
object on the side is the
same as doing inline styles.

475
00:30:34,770 --> 00:30:38,950
Actually, it's usually preferred
to do StyleSheet.create

476
00:30:38,950 --> 00:30:46,350
like this because it's more
efficient when the app is

477
00:30:46,350 --> 00:30:49,270
rerendering multiple times.

478
00:30:49,270 --> 00:30:54,350
So if you can, usually I
prefer to make a style sheet

479
00:30:54,350 --> 00:30:57,150
on the side like this.

480
00:30:57,150 --> 00:31:00,590
OK, so let's look at
modifying this page further

481
00:31:00,590 --> 00:31:03,710
with other components.

482
00:31:03,710 --> 00:31:08,370
You won't need to worry about
memorizing how to do that.

483
00:31:08,370 --> 00:31:13,870
What I want you to focus on is
understanding the concept of,

484
00:31:13,870 --> 00:31:16,450
there are components
inside of components.

485
00:31:16,450 --> 00:31:20,670
And that's how you
make up your page.

486
00:31:20,670 --> 00:31:25,630
You will be able to
just, say, go to your AI

487
00:31:25,630 --> 00:31:29,860
and get help building
whatever you want it to be.

488
00:31:29,860 --> 00:31:37,080
So let's say I have
a new Expo App.

489
00:31:37,080 --> 00:31:40,760
This is my--

490
00:31:40,760 --> 00:31:44,660
I don't even need to tell it
